<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Fix for Visual Studio 2026 (VC v180): "MSB4018 The SetEnv task failed unexpectedly /
  System.ArgumentException: Environment variable name or value is too long."

  Unreal projects are NMake/Makefile .vcxproj that delegate the build to
  UnrealBuildTool (Build.bat). UBT writes the FULL engine+project+plugin include
  list (35k+ chars for an editor target) into <IncludePath>. VS 2026's C++ targets
  add a new step, AddExternalIncludDirectoriesToPaths, that pushes that list into the
  INCLUDE environment variable; once it passes the Windows 32,767-char env-var limit
  the SetEnv task throws and the build stops BEFORE it ever runs UBT.

  The real UE build (UnrealBuildTool) passes include paths to the compiler DIRECTLY
  and does NOT read this INCLUDE env var, and IntelliSense uses <NMakeIncludeSearchPath>
  (not <IncludePath>). So we reset <IncludePath>/<ExternalIncludePath> to the short
  default toolchain includes purely to keep the SetEnv step under the limit. This does
  NOT affect the compiled result or IntelliSense.

  MSBuild imports this automatically (it walks up from the generated
  Intermediate\ProjectFiles\*.vcxproj to the project root), and it survives a
  "Generate Visual Studio project files" regeneration because it lives outside
  the regenerated Intermediate\ folder. The scaffolder copies it into every new
  project because it sits at the template root.
-->
<Project>
  <PropertyGroup>
    <IncludePath>$(VC_IncludePath);$(WindowsSDK_IncludePath)</IncludePath>
    <ExternalIncludePath>$(VC_IncludePath);$(WindowsSDK_IncludePath)</ExternalIncludePath>
  </PropertyGroup>
</Project>
